# Citation File Format: https://citation-file-format.github.io/
# Run `cffconvert -i CITATION.cff -f bibtex` to get a BibTeX entry.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Riprap: cited flood and heat briefings for New York City places, from public data"
type: software
message: >-
  If you use Riprap in research, reporting or applied civic work, please
  cite it.
authors:
  - given-names: Adam
    family-names: Rahman
    name-particle: Munawar
    email: msrahmanadam@gmail.com
    affiliation: "Independent"
repository-code: "https://github.com/msradam/riprap"
url: "https://msradam.github.io/riprap/"
abstract: >-
  Riprap gathers public flood and heat evidence for a New York City
  address, a question about one, or a community district, and cites every
  sentence to its source. NYC is the production deployment; Chicago,
  Seattle and Albany are experimental deployments on the same code. Code
  writes each sentence from one public record, and a question is answered
  by rules over its words. An optional open Granite model, off by default,
  may route a question the rules do not match and may only choose among
  the existing sentences; it never writes a sentence, and no yes or no
  stands on its word. One experimental
  model fine-tuned by the author (a land-cover model) adds sentences,
  always labelled experimental, with its limits and tested accuracy. A
  second, a surge forecast for the Battery, lost to a one-line rule on
  held-out data and is out of default briefings unless a server opts in;
  a third, a satellite water layer, was tested and retired. Thirteen disclosure checks drawn from
  FEMA, IPCC AR6, TCFD, ASTM E1527-21, EPA/CDC CERC, the AP Stylebook
  and the SPJ Code report on every briefing. Riprap reports evidence; it
  does not give advice or make regulatory flood determinations. The
  code is Apache-2.0. FloodNet data and the sentences and counts derived
  from it are CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, not Apache-2.0 (see NOTICE).
keywords:
  - civic-tech
  - flood-risk
  - climate-exposure
  - citation-grounded
  - grounded-llm
  - open-data
  - socrata
  - fema-nfhl
  - noaa-coops
  - npcc4
# The licence of the code. FloodNet data and the sentences and counts
# Riprap derives from it are CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and are not covered by it:
# see "FloodNet-derived content" in NOTICE.
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.8.0
date-released: "2026-10-01"

# How Riprap depends on prior work. We cite the data sources Riprap
# composes in each per-pebble manifest's `provenance` block (see
# deployments/<city>/manifests/*.yaml); the items below are the
# model and methodology references.
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Granite 4.1 Language Models"
    authors:
      - name: "IBM Research"
    year: 2025
    notes: "An optional LLM, off by default, that may route a question the rules do not recognise and choose among existing sentences. The public gallery is made by the rules alone, with no model. Apache-2.0. https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite"
  - type: software
    title: "Granite TimeSeries TTM r2 (Tiny Time Mixers)"
    authors:
      - name: "IBM Research"
    year: 2024
    notes: "Base of the experimental Battery surge forecast, msradam/Granite-TTM-r2-Battery-Surge, out of default briefings since 2026-10-05. Apache-2.0. https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite/granite-timeseries-ttm-r2"
  - type: software
    title: "Prithvi-EO-2.0: A Versatile Multi-Temporal Foundation Model for Earth Observation Applications"
    authors:
      - name: "NASA IMPACT and IBM Research"
    year: 2024
    notes: "Base of the satellite water layer, msradam/Prithvi-EO-2.0-NYC-Pluvial, tested and retired from the app in October 2026. Apache-2.0. arXiv:2412.02732"
  - type: software
    title: "TerraMind 1.0"
    authors:
      - name: "IBM Research and the European Space Agency"
    year: 2025
    notes: "Base of the experimental NYC land-cover model, fine-tuned in this repository on NYC Land Cover 2017 (scripts/train_cover.py). Apache-2.0. https://huggingface.co/ibm-esa-geospatial/TerraMind-1.0-base"
  - type: software
    title: "riprap-models: reproduction of three NYC fine-tuned foundation models"
    authors:
      - given-names: Adam
        family-names: Rahman
        name-particle: Munawar
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/msradam/riprap-models"
  - type: article
    title: "New York City Panel on Climate Change 2024 Report (NPCC4)"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Rosenzweig"
        given-names: "Cynthia"
    year: 2024
    journal: "Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences"
    volume: 1539
    doi: "10.1111/nyas.15116"
